 { Alexander Morozevich vs. Shakhriyar Mamedyarov, Beijing 2013 Learning objectives: 1. Black needs to achieve either ...c6-c5 or ...e6-e5 in order to release his position. 2. This game illustrates how White can break through Black’s passive defence if neither of these breaks is achieved. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. d4 c6 6. O-O d5 7. Qb3 dxc4 8. Qxc4 Qd5 { Having a half-point lead in the Beijing Grand Prix tournament, Mamedyarov chose this rare sideline, offering to get the queens off the board. } 9. Nbd2 $5 { And surprisingly, Morozevich (a well-known tactician) responds with another rare move (a novelty after only 9 moves) accepting entry into an endgame. } ( { Previously avoiding the exchange with } 9. Qa4 $5 { had been played with success. } ) 
Qxc4 10. Nxc4 { This is a key moment in the game and deserves some comment. With the queens off the board, Black is very unlikely to face a mating attack like we saw in the previous game. But does this mean Black can secure a draw? Not necessarily. Neutralizing White’s space advantage is not easy even without queens, and the key question is whether Black will be able to free his position with ...c6-c5 or ...e7-e5 in order to dispute White’s central dominance. If that is not the case, White will enjoy a lasting positional advantage and the defensive task will require Black to constantly calculate how to defend against possible breaks, giving him little hope in a practical game where errors cannot be completely avoided. } Be6 ( { The alternative } 10... Nbd7 { does not seem to change things much after: } 11. b3 Nb6 ( { Black cannot break in the centre with } 11... c5 $2 12. Ba3 $1 { as Black will have a serious struggle to complete his development without losing material, for example } Nd5 13. Rac1 cxd4 14. Nxd4 Bxd4 15. Bxd5 Bf6 16. Rfd1 $16 ) 
12. Na5 $1 { Avoiding the exchange of pieces, and preventing Black from developing his bishop due to the pressure on b7. } Rd8 13. Ba3 Nbd5 14. Rac1 $14 { This is similar to the game. } ) 
11. b3 Bd5 ( { The alternative } 11... Ne4 12. Ba3 Re8 ( { The break } 12... c5 $2 { fails due to } 13. Nfe5 $1 { ± } ) 
13. Rac1 { is similar to the game. } ) 
 ( { The try } 11... Bxc4 12. bxc4 Ne4 { Hoping for ...c6-c5, is met by } 13. Rb1 $1 b6 ( { Or } 13... Nc3 $6 14. Rxb7 Nxe2 15. Kh1 Nxd4 16. Nxd4 Bxd4 17. Rxe7 $16 ) 
14. Bf4 $14 { followed by Ne5 with a comfortable edge. } ) 
12. Ba3 Re8 13. Rac1 Nbd7 14. Na5 { The knight will be ideally located on a5, pressuring the b7-pawn and preventing ...b7-b6 due to Nxc6. } Rab8 15. Rfd1 $14 { It becomes clear that Black will not easily be able to dispute White’s centre, thus falling into a permanently inferior position. } Bf8 ( { The break } 15... e5 $2 { fails due to } 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Rxe5 18. Bd6 { winning the exchange. } ) 
 ( { Nor does it help to play: } 15... Ne4 16. Nd2 { White needs to trade some pieces in order to be able to push the desired e2-e4 gaining central control. } Ndf6 ( { Or } 16... Nxd2 17. Bxd5 cxd5 18. Rxd2 $14 { when the character of the game has changed, but White retains his advantage by controlling the only open file. } ) 
17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. f3 Nd6 19. e4 Be6 { White dominates and may continue to improve his position with } 20. Bc5 Nc8 21. f4 Nb6 22. h3 $14 { intending to gain even more space with g3-g4. } ) 
 ( { The attempt } 15... Bxf3 16. Bxf3 Bf8 { intending to follow up with ...e7-e5 can be met by } 17. Bb2 e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Bg2 { where the pair of bishops and excellent piece coordination secure an advantage for White, for example: } Bb4 20. Nc4 Nxc4 21. Bxf6 Nd6 22. Kf1 $14 ) 
16. Ne5 Bxg2 ( { Keeping more pieces on the board with } 16... Be6 $6 { is unlikely to help Black due to his lack of space. } ) 
17. Kxg2 e6 ( { Worse is } 17... Nxe5 18. dxe5 Nd5 19. e4 Nb6 20. Rc3 { followed by Rcd3 controlling the only open file. } ) 
18. Bxf8 Kxf8 19. Nec4 Ke7 ( { But not } 19... e5 $2 { due to } 20. Nd6 Re7 21. Naxb7 { and wins. } ) 
20. b4 { Preventing the break ...c6-c5. } Rec8 ( 20... Nb6 21. Ne5 Nfd7 22. Naxc6 bxc6 23. Nxc6 Kd6 24. Nxb8 Rxb8 25. e4 $16 ) 
21. a3 g5 $1 { A good decision, as Black needs to claim some space in order not to be asphyxiated by White’s potential expansion with e2-e4, f2-f4, h2-h3 and g3-g4. } ( { White should constantly calculate liberation attempts, such as } 21... b6 $6 22. Nb3 c5 $6 { but fortunately for White, in this case, this attempt is refuted by } 23. dxc5 bxc5 24. Nca5 $1 { ± winning material. } ) 
22. Nb2 { A prophylactic move, preventing freeing attempts with ...b7-b6 followed by ...c6-c5 as mentioned in the previous note. } Ne8 23. Nd3 { Note how both knights are ideally arranged to pressure Black’s queenside and prevent liberation. } f5 24. h4 $1 { Gaining control of the f4-square. } g4 ( { But not } 24... gxh4 $2 25. Rh1 $1 Nef6 ( { Even worse is } 25... hxg3 $2 26. Rxh7 Kd8 27. Nf4 ) 
26. Rxh4 $16 { and the h7-pawn has become a serious weakness. } ) 
 ( { A bad idea would be } 24... h6 $6 25. hxg5 hxg5 26. Rh1 Nef6 27. Ne5 { when only White will take advantage of the open h-file. } ) 
25. Nf4 Nef6 26. Rc2 Nd5 27. Nd3 Rf8 28. e3 Rfe8 29. Re1 { Speculating on a potential e3-e4. Note how Black constantly needs to calculate how to defend against potential threats, while White can choose his moves almost randomly. A practical strategy for White is to continuously present Black with potential threats, but without carrying out any of them until the right time has come. Often White will continue to use this strategy until Black commits a serious mistake, or finds himself in time trouble, and only then will White embark on decisive action. } N5f6 { Preventing e3-e4. } ( { To illustrate how delicate Black’s position is, note how the natural } 29... Rf8 { falls into trouble after } 30. Rb2 $1 { (threatening Nc5 winning a pawn) } Rfc8 31. e4 $1 fxe4 32. Rxe4 $16 { and Black is on the verge of losing. } ) 
30. Rb1 { Threatening Nc5 winning a pawn. } Rec8 31. Rbc1 Rd8 ( { More stubborn was } 31... Nb6 { . } ) 
32. a4 $1 { Preparing a break with b4-b5 in order to invade Black’s camp via the c-file. } Ne4 $2 { Mamedyarov miscalculates in an already very difficult situation. } ( { The correct defence was } 32... Ne8 $1 33. b5 cxb5 34. axb5 Nd6 35. Rc7 Nxb5 36. Rxb7 Rxb7 37. Nxb7 Rb8 38. Na5 $16 { though Black’s position is full of weaknesses. } ) 
33. b5 cxb5 34. Rc7 $1 { This would have been prevented if Black’s knight had been on e8. } Nd6 ( 34... bxa4 $2 { loses to } 35. Nxb7 Re8 36. Ne5 Nef6 37. Nc6 { . } ) 
35. Nc5 bxa4 $6 ( { A better defence was } 35... Ne8 36. Rxb7 Rxb7 37. Ncxb7 Ra8 38. axb5 { though Black’s position is practically lost. } ) 
36. Ncxb7 Nxb7 37. Nxb7 Rf8 38. R1c6 $1 { Black’s position falls apart due to the threat of 39. Nc5. } Rf7 39. Nc5 Ke8 40. Rxe6+ Kd8 41. Rec6 { Black resigns as the a7- and a4-pawns will soon be lost. Final remarks 1. If Black wishes to equalize, it is absolutely necessary to find a break by either ...e6-e5 or ...c6-c5. 2. The endgame starting on move 19 was nearly lost on a practical level. The reason is that Black could not create any counterplay or find any way to equalize. Meanwhile, White could move his pieces around the entire board, speculating with multiple threats. Even Super-GMs like Mamedyarov cannot avoid decisive mistakes under such circumstances. } 
 1-0

