 { Jon Ludvig Hammer – Magnus Carlsen, Sandnes 2013 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of a position in which Black is likely to achieve the ...c6-c5 break. 2. After the break is achieved, Black has to parry some threats. But once these threats are neutralized, it is Black who plays for the advantage. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. g3 dxc4 5. Bg2 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 a5 7. Qc2 Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 c6 9. a4 ( { The alternative } 9. Ne5 { can lead to a complicated position after } b5 10. a4 $5 { when White has pressure on the queenside and centre, in compensation for Black’s extra pawn. Or } 10. Nxc6 Qc7 $11 ) 
Ne4 10. Qc2 Nd6 { Black will not give up the c4-pawn without trading another piece to alleviate his space problem. } 11. Nbd2 Na6 12. Nxc4 Nb4 13. Nxd6+ Qxd6 14. Qd2 { We have reached the position of interest, and the reader should pay close attention to the differences between this game and the previous one. In the current game, two minor pieces are off the board already, decreasing Black’s space problem. In addition, the moves a2-a4 and ...a7-a5 greatly favour Black, as they give a strong square for the knight on b4 and have eliminated White’s chances to expand on the queenside in the future with the usual b2-b4. Finally, Black has a concrete plan, which is ...b7-b6, ...Ba6 (or ...Bb7) followed by placing the rooks in the centre and pushing ...c6-c5. Unlike the previous game, White lacks pieces to prevent such liberation. For all the above reasons, the reader should understand that Black has chosen correctly when he decided to play this pawn structure. } O-O 15. O-O Rd8 16. Rfd1 b6 17. Qc3 ( { The alternative } 17. Ne5 { is not enough to prevent Black’s liberation either: } Ba6 18. Rac1 Rac8 19. Nc4 Bxc4 20. Rxc4 c5 { and Black has fully equalized. } ) 
Ba6 18. Rd2 ( { If } 18. e4 $6 { Black is ready to break in the centre with } c5 $1 { ⩱ taking the lead, since } 19. dxc5 $2 { loses to } Qxd1 20. Rxd1 Rxd1 21. Ne1 Nc2 $1 22. Qxc2 Rxe1 23. Bf1 Bxf1 { . } ) 
Rac8 19. Rad1 { Placing both rooks on the d-file is correct, as White needs to fight for the initiative once the ...c6-c5 break comes. } Qe7 { Preparing c6-c5. } ( { Of course not } 19... c5 $2 20. dxc5 { . } ) 
20. h4 c5 { Black achieves the desired break, disputing White’s control of the centre. } ( { Also possible was } 20... Rd6 $5 { in order to further prepare the ...c6-c5 break. } ) 
21. dxc5 Rd5 $5 { An interesting intermediate move, preparing ...Rcxc5. } ( { Another option was: } 21... Rxd2 22. Qxd2 Qxc5 23. Ne5 $5 { This is White’s only chance to fight for an advantage, but after } Nd5 { there is nothing special for White, for example } 24. Bxd5 Qxd5 25. Qxd5 exd5 26. e3 f6 27. Nd7 Rc2 { with a level game. } ) 
22. Rxd5 ( { A good alternative was: } 22. Ng5 $5 Rcxc5 23. Qe3 Rxd2 24. Qxd2 { (threatening Qd8) } Rc8 25. Qd7 Qxd7 26. Rxd7 Bxe2 27. Nxf7 $14 ) 
 ( { PH: Magnus took a bit of risk, as } 22. Ne5 $1 { would've been good for White. It's complicated to calculate, though: } Bxe2 23. Bxd5 Bxd1 { . Here two white pawns are hanging and it looks good for Black at first, but White has } 24. Bc4 $1 Bxa4 25. cxb6 { . White is not winning, but is somewhat better. } ) 
Nxd5 23. Qe5 Qxc5 { Black has fully equalized. } 24. Bh3 { Threatening Bxe6. } ( { If White tries } 24. Nd4 { threatening Nxe6, Black equalizes with the forced line } Nc3 $1 25. Qxc5 bxc5 26. bxc3 cxd4 27. cxd4 Bxe2 ) 
 ( { Or } 24. Ng5 Nf6 $1 25. Qxc5 bxc5 26. e3 Rb8 { with mutual chances. } ) 
Re8 25. Nd4 { Reinforcing the threat on e6. } ( { Alternatively, } 25. Ng5 Nf6 26. Qxc5 bxc5 { with a level endgame } ) 
Kf8 { A curious move. } 26. Bf1 ( { Or } 26. e4 Nb4 27. Qxc5 bxc5 28. Nb5 Ke7 { with a balanced endgame. } ) 
Rc8 27. Nb5 $2 { This mistake is hard to understand. } ( { Better was } 27. Nf5 Nf6 28. Qxc5 Rxc5 29. Rd8 Ne8 30. Nd6 Ke7 31. Rxe8 Kxd6 32. Rb8 Rc6 { with approximate equality, though I find Black’s position slightly preferable since his king is very close to White’s weakened queenside. } ) 
 ( { On the other hand } 27. e4 $2 { is bad: } Bxf1 28. exd5 ( { or } 28. Rxf1 Nf6 29. Qxc5 Rxc5 $17 ) 
Bc4 $17 { winning a pawn. } ) 
Bxb5 28. axb5 Nf6 $1 { Now White’s position becomes very difficult due to the weak b2- and b5-pawns. } ( { The pawn is untouchable: } 28... Qxb5 $2 29. e4 { when White wins the knight after } Qa4 30. b3 $1 { (the only move) } Qxb3 31. Rd3 { followed by exd5 since here } Nc3 $2 { allows mate by: } 32. Qd6 Kg8 ( 32... Ke8 33. Qd7 ) 
33. Qd8 Rxd8 34. Rxd8 ) 
29. Qd6+ ( 29. Qf4 Qxb5 $17 ) 
Ke8 ( { Also possible was } 29... Qxd6 30. Rxd6 Nd5 31. e4 Ke7 32. e5 Rc1 $17 { followed by ...Rb1xb2 when the a5-pawn should decide the game. } ) 
30. Qd3 Qd5 $1 { ∓ Forcing the trade of queens under ideal circumstances, as Black’s king is already closer to the battlefield, and White’s b-pawns are very weak. } 31. Qxd5 exd5 32. e4 $5 ( { White is desperately looking for counterplay. Passive defence with } 32. Rd2 { does not help: } Ke7 33. e3 Rc1 34. f3 Kd6 35. Kf2 Kc5 $17 { when White’s queenside weaknesses and Black’s strong king should tell. } ) 
Rc2 $5 { A good practical decision, depriving White of counterplay. } ( { Technically } 32... dxe4 { works, but White gains some counterplay with } 33. Rd6 { . } ) 
33. e5 $6 ( { Preferable was } 33. exd5 Rxb2 $17 { when Black’s a5-pawn is much more dangerous than the isolated d5-pawn. } ) 
Ne4 34. Rxd5 Rxb2 $19 { The a5-pawn now decides the game. } 35. Rd4 Rb4 36. Rd1 ( { Instead } 36. Rxb4 $2 axb4 { loses immediately, since White must give up his bishop for the pawn. } ) 
a4 37. Bg2 Nc3 38. Bc6+ Ke7 39. Rd7+ Ke6 40. Ra7 Kxe5 { White resigns since the a4-pawn cannot be stopped without great material loss. Final remarks 1. The main message of this game is that White cannot really hope for an advantage if he is unable to prevent a ...c6-c5 break. 2. A crucial role was played by the weakening move a2-a4 and Black’s knight on b4, which substantially limited the coordination of White’s pieces. For example, on moves 17-19 White would have liked to play Rac1, but could not due to ...Na2 winning an exchange. } 
 0-1

