 { Levon Aronian – David Navara, Wijk aan Zee 2012 Learning objective: This game is an illustration of how Black may carry out the ...e6-e5 break. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Qb3 Nb6 6. d4 Bg7 7. e4 Bg4 { A logical move, undermining White’s centre due to the threat of ...Bxf3 followed by ...Bxd4. } ( { Instead taking the pawn with } 7... Bxd4 $2 { is simply unjustified, for example } 8. Bh6 $5 { prevents Black from castling and can be followed by Rd1 with strong pressure. } ) 
8. Bb5+ c6 9. Ng5 O-O 10. Be2 Bxe2 11. Nxe2 Na6 { This move became the main line after this game. Developing the knight via a6 facilitates the key manoeuvre ...Qd6-b4 attempting to trade queens. } ( { Previously } 11... N8d7 { had been played in the game Gordon – Bok, Germany 2011, where White gained an initiative after } 12. Qh3 ( { Another option was } 11... Na6 11. e5 $5 { ⩲ } ) 
h6 13. Nf3 h5 14. e5 $5 { with the potential threat e5-e6. } ) 
 ( 11... Bxd4 $2 { is not possible due to the brute-force response } 12. Qh3 h5 13. g4 $1 { followed by gxh5 with an attack. } ) 
12. Be3 ( { An alternative was } 12. Qh3 $5 h6 13. Nf3 h5 14. g4 { with great complications, as in the game Navara – Hammer, Tromsø 2013. } ) 
Qd6 { This is Black’s clearest route to equality, aiming to trade queens before liberating his position with ...e7-e5. } ( { The alternative } 12... e5 13. dxe5 Bxe5 14. f4 Bg7 15. O-O { gives White a very slightly preferable position, as he controls the centre better and may potentially seize the initiative with e4-e5 or f4-f5. } ) 
13. O-O Qb4 $1 { A key moment during the game. Simple calculation should allow White to realize he is no longer able to prevent Black’s successful liberation with ...e7-e5 (or ...c6-c5) therefore it is necessary to accurately calculate what position will be best to neutralize Black’s initiative once this break is accomplished. } ( { The option } 13... c5 { is not advisable due to } 14. Rad1 cxd4 15. Bxd4 $14 ) 
 ( { But } 13... e5 { was a reasonable alternative, for example } 14. Rad1 exd4 15. Bxd4 Bxd4 ) 
14. Qxb4 $6 { Allowing the knight to come to b4 makes White’s position quite vulnerable due to the positional threat ...Nc2xe3. } ( { It is possible that Aronian was dissatisfied with } 14. Qc2 { due to } e5 ( { Or Black can insist on the queen trade with } 14. Qxb4 Qc4 $5 ) 
15. Rad1 exd4 16. Nxd4 Rad8 { when Black’s position is very slightly preferable due to the pressure on the centre. } ) 
Nxb4 15. Rfc1 ( { If } 15. Nf3 { to prevent ...e7-e5, then } Nc2 16. Rac1 Nxe3 17. fxe3 e5 $1 { when Black’s position is preferable, and of course White does not wish to reply: } 18. Nxe5 $2 Bxe5 19. dxe5 Rae8 $17 ) 
 ( { Of course not } 15. Rac1 $2 Nxa2 { . } ) 
e5 $1 { A timely liberation for Black. } 16. Nf3 ( 16. dxe5 Bxe5 17. Bc5 Nd3 $1 18. Bxf8 Nxc1 19. Rxc1 Rxf8 $15 ) 
exd4 17. Bxd4 Rfe8 { The e4-pawn is suddenly a weakness. } 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Ng3 $6 { Bringing the pieces too far away from the queenside. } ( { If } 19. Nc3 Rad8 20. Rd1 Nd3 { Black’s position is slightly preferable. } ) 
Rad8 20. Ne1 ( { The logical } 20. Rd1 $6 { fighting for the d-file fails due to } Na4 $1 { attacking the b2-pawn; after } 21. Rab1 Rxd1 22. Rxd1 Re7 { protecting the seventh rank } ( { But not } 22. Ne1 Nxb2 $2 23. Rd7 { with counterplay. } ) 
23. Rb1 Rd7 $17 { and White is about to lose a pawn. } ) 
Rd2 ( { The alternative was } 20... Rd7 $5 { (threatening ...h5-h4 followed by ...Rxe4) } 21. f3 Red8 { controlling the only open file with a big advantage. } ) 
21. a3 Nd3 $6 ( { Inaccurate. Better was } 21... Na6 22. b4 Nc7 { when the queenside pawns are very weak. } ) 
22. Nxd3 $6 { White returns the favour and now his position becomes very difficult to hold due to the weak pawns on b2 and e4. } ( { As GM Krasenkow suggests, } 22. Nf1 $1 { was the right defence: } Nxc1 23. Nxd2 Rd8 24. Rxc1 Rxd2 25. Rc2 { and White seems to be holding though Black’s position is still preferable. } ) 
Rxd3 23. Rab1 ( { Slightly better was } 23. f3 { but after } Red8 { Black controls the game. } ) 
Re5 $1 { A good move, planning the transfer ...Re5-b5 pressing against the b2-pawn and forcing White to give up a pawn. } 24. Rd1 Rxd1+ 25. Rxd1 Rb5 26. b4 Nc4 27. Rd3 a5 $1 { The key move. } 28. bxa5 ( 28. Rb3 { does not help, as } c5 { wins. } ) 
Rxa5 29. f4 Rxa3 { Black’s strategy has been a success, but the game is not over yet due to: } 30. Rd7 $1 { Aronian has not given up and he now embarks on some sharp counterplay. } b5 { The race begins. } 31. e5 b4 ( { If } 31... Kf8 32. Ne4 $1 { and the game suddenly becomes unclear due to the threats Ng5 and Nf6. } ) 
32. e6 b3 33. Rxf7+ ( { The alternative was } 33. e7 $5 b2 34. e8=Q $11 b1=Q 35. Nf1 Qf5 $17 { though Black’s position is very promising due to the extra pawn and White’s slightly more exposed king. } ) 
Kh6 34. Rb7 b2 35. Kf2 Ra7 ( { Or } 35... Ra2 $5 { transposing into the game continuation. } ) 
36. Rb8 Ra8 37. Rb7 Ra2 38. Ne2 Nd6 $1 39. e7 $1 ( { But not } 39. Rb8 $2 Kg7 { followed by ...Kf6 winning. } ) 
Nxb7 40. e8=Q b1=Q { The game is technically won for Black though White still managed to find some resources against Black’s awkwardly located king. I leave the rest of the game without much comment, though it is interesting to play over. } 41. Qf8+ Kh5 42. Qe7 Qb6+ 43. Kf3 Qb3+ 44. Kf2 Rxe2+ 45. Qxe2+ ( { A nice variation suggested by GM Krasenkow is } 45. Kxe2 $1 h6 46. h3 Qg3 $1 { (apparently the only winning method) } 47. Qe5 g5 48. Qe8 Kh4 49. Qxc6 Qxf4 $1 50. Qxb7 Kg3 $1 { and the queen endgame is won as the g2- and h3-pawns are lost. } ) 
Kh6 46. g4 Nd8 47. Qe7 Ne6 48. Qh4+ Kg7 49. Qe7+ Kg8 50. Qe8+ Nf8 51. Qxc6 Qe6 52. Qf3 Nd7 53. h3 Kf7 54. Qc3 Qd5 55. Kg3 Nc5 56. Kh4 h6 57. Qh8 g5+ 58. fxg5 hxg5+ 59. Kh5 Qe4 60. Kxg5 Ne6+ ( { White resigned in view of the forced checkmate after: } 60... Ne6+ 60. Kh4 Qe1 62. Kh5 Ng7 63. Kg5 Qd2 64. Kh4 Qf2 65. Kg5 Qf6 { Final remarks 1. The break ...e6-e5 is desirable in order to disrupt White’s centre. 2. In addition, if White’s pieces are underdeveloped, then this break can help Black play for an advantage by penetrating down the d-file. } ) 

