 { Wang Yue – Magnus Carlsen, Medias 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White’s d4-pawn could become vulnerable, and how Black makes use of it. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bd2 Bg7 6. e4 Nb6 7. Be3 { An opening book would say this is really a Grünfeld position and this is true. However, from a positional standpoint, the structure and character of this game more closely resembles the Queen’s Gambit Accepted. } O-O 8. Bb5 { This standard opening move prevents Black from developing a knight to c6. } Be6 9. Nge2 c6 10. Bd3 Nc4 11. Bxc4 Bxc4 12. O-O Nd7 13. Qd2 Qa5 14. Rfd1 Rad8 15. Bh6 Bxe2 16. Nxe2 Qxd2 17. Bxd2 Nb6 { We have reached the position we are truly interested in. The reader should notice how well Black has conducted this game. He managed to trade two minor pieces and the queen, solving the space problem, and now is ready to exert pressure against the d4-pawn. White’s pieces are poorly located to defend the vulnerable centre. } 18. Bc3 { Other bishop moves only further risk the d4-pawn. } ( { If } 18. e5 { , blocking the bishop on g7 to protect the d4-pawn, then after } f6 $5 19. f4 Nc4 20. Bc3 Rd7 $15 { Black enjoys a pleasant positional edge. } ) 
 ( { The try } 18. Be3 { is met by: } Nc4 $1 19. b3 Nxe3 20. fxe3 e5 $1 { ⩱ } ) 
Rd7 { Preparing to double rooks in order to increase the pressure against d4. } ( { A strong alternative was: } 18... Na4 $5 19. Rd2 f5 $1 20. exf5 ( { Supporting the centre with } 19... Rd7 19. f3 { is not possible due to } Bh6 $1 21. Rd3 e5 $1 { ∓ creating strong threats, for example } 22. Rad1 $2 ( { or } 21... Rd7 21. d5 cxd5 23. exd5 Rfe8 $17 ) 
fxe4 23. fxe4 exd4 24. Nxd4 Nc5 25. Rh3 Bg7 { winning material by force. } ) 
Rxf5 21. Rad1 Rfd5 $15 { With definite pressure. } ) 
19. b3 ( { PH: A good alternative for White is } 19. a4 { , intending a5, but Black can just ignore it and double with } Rfd8 { , and then } 20. a5 Nc4 { , probably followed by ...Nd6-b5 with strong pressure on d4. } ) 
f5 $5 { Challenging White’s central control. } ( { Another option was } 19... Rfd8 { pressuring the d4-pawn and now: } 20. Rd2 Na8 $1 { A key manoeuvre. } 21. Rad1 Nc7 { (heading to either b5 or e6) } 22. f3 f5 $5 { ⩱ gives better chances to Black due to White’s vulnerable central pawns. } ) 
20. f3 { Maintaining control of the d5-square. } ( { If } 20. exf5 Rxf5 { White’s centre is quite vulnerable, for example: } 21. Rac1 Nd5 22. Bd2 Rf8 23. g3 Nc7 24. Be3 Rfd8 $15 { followed by ...Nb5 and potential breaks ...e7-e5 or ...c6-c5. } ) 
 ( { PH: A third way of responding is } 20. e5 { , which looks conceptually bad, but this type of structure is not unheard of in the Grunfeld. The point is that after } Nd5 21. f4 $5 { Black's bishop is also completely blocked in and White stabilizes a bit. After 20.e5, Black should probably play } g5 $5 { , preventing f4 from White in the first place. } ) 
Rfd8 21. Re1 fxe4 ( { Now it is too late for } 21... Na8 { hoping for ...Nc7-b5, because of } 22. d5 $1 Bxc3 23. Nxc3 cxd5 24. Nxd5 { with a level game. } ) 
22. fxe4 e5 $1 { This is a break to be remembered. It forces the creation of an isolated e-pawn. } 23. dxe5 Rd3 { Probably not the most precise. } ( { Slightly better was } 23... Re8 $5 24. Rad1 Bxe5 25. Bxe5 Rxe5 26. Nc3 Kf7 $15 { when the e4-pawn is somewhat vulnerable. } ) 
24. g3 $2 { A severe imprecision allowing Black to take the lead. } ( { White could have held on to approximate equality after } 24. Bb2 Re8 25. Rad1 Re3 26. Nc1 Rxe1 27. Rxe1 Nd7 28. Nd3 Nxe5 { . } ) 
Nd7 $1 25. e6 $6 { Worsening the situation. } ( { A better continuation was } 25. Ba5 Nxe5 ( { or } 25. e6 b6 26. Nf4 $1 ) 
26. Bxd8 Nf3 27. Kf2 Nxe1 28. Rxe1 Rxd8 $15 { though Black has good chances to fight for the win. } ) 
Bxc3 26. Nxc3 Ne5 $1 { Now Black has obtained the kind of position one dreams of when playing ...f7-f5 followed by ...e7-e5. The e4-pawn is a clear weakness and Black will follow with ...Kf8-e7xe6 with a clear advantage. } 27. Red1 Kf8 28. Rac1 ( 28. Rxd3 Rxd3 29. Na4 { does not help because of the simple: } Ke7 30. Nc5 Rd2 $1 31. Nxb7 Nf3 32. Kf1 Nxh2 33. Ke1 Nf3 34. Kf1 Kxe6 $17 ) 
Ke7 29. Rxd3 Rxd3 30. Rc2 Nf3+ 31. Kf1 Nd4 32. Rc1 Kxe6 { White’s position is desperate despite having equal material, because of the chronic weakness of the e4-pawn. } 33. Rd1 { Other options are worse. } ( { Another try was } 33. Ke1 Ke5 34. Nd1 b6 ( { But not } 34. Rd1 Kxe4 $4 35. Nf2 ) 
35. Rc4 c5 36. Nf2 Rf3 $17 { and Black will progress slowly with ...h7-h5, ...g6-g5-g4, ...a7-a5, etc. White’s position is hopeless. } ) 
Rxc3 ( { An interesting option was } 33... Rf3 $5 34. Ke1 ( { Or } 33... Rxc3 33. Kg2 Rxc3 35. Rxd4 Rc2 36. Kf3 Rxa2 $19 ) 
Ke5 35. Ne2 c5 36. Nxd4 cxd4 $17 { with a practically winning position. } ) 
34. Rxd4 Rc2 35. a4 ( { As Rogozenko suggests, the try } 35. Ra4 { does not help because after } a6 36. h4 Ke5 { White is in zugzwang and lost. For example } 37. Ke1 Rg2 ( { Or } 37. Kg1 Re2 ) 
 ( { Or, worst of all, } 37. Rb4 $2 b5 { followed by ...c6-c5. } ) 
 ) 
Rxh2 { Black has a healthy extra pawn, and won this endgame without difficulty. } 36. a5 Rh5 37. b4 Rh2 38. a6 b5 $1 { Keeping the queenside pawns healthy. } 39. e5 Ra2 40. Rd6+ Kxe5 41. Rxc6 Kf5 42. Rc7 Rxa6 43. Rxh7 Kg4 44. Kf2 Ra2+ 45. Ke3 g5 46. Rg7 Rb2 47. Rxa7 Kxg3 { Final remarks 1. In the endgame, once e3-e4 has been played, the d4-pawn can become an object of attack. 2. The reader should remember that pressuring the d4-pawn will not always work. It works particularly well in this game because Black’s bishop was on g7, and because White’s pieces were poorly placed to begin with. 3. The break ...f7-f5 is an important move in Black’s strategy, undermining the control of the d5-square. 4. The idea of ...f7-f5, followed by trading pawns on e4 and playing ...e7-e5, is thematic and strategically sound, as it turns the e4-pawn into a permanent weakness. } 
 0-1

